From: Pedro Baltazar <pedrobtz@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Split the compile error texts below 4095 characters

compile_error_texts is one string literal of about 5,700 characters. ISO
C99 only requires compilers to support 4,095, so clang -pedantic, which
CRAN's checks use, warns "string literal of length 5686 exceeds maximum
length 4095 that ISO C99 compilers are required to support", and R CMD
check reports that as a significant compiler warning.

The list is split at error 60 into two literals, and
pcre2_get_error_message() looks in the second for numbers from 60 on.
Every message and its number are unchanged.

Local to zuregex; to be offered upstream at
https://github.com/PCRE2Project/pcre2.
---
--- a/src/pcre2_error.c
+++ b/src/pcre2_error.c
@@ -133,6 +133,16 @@
   "(?R (recursive pattern call) must be followed by a closing parenthesis\0"
   /* "an argument is not allowed for (*ACCEPT), (*FAIL), or (*COMMIT)\0" */
   "obsolete error (should not occur)\0"  /* Was the above */
+  ;
+
+/* zuregex: the list is split here, at error 60, so that neither literal
+exceeds the 4095 characters ISO C99 requires compilers to support; CRAN
+compiles with -pedantic, which warns about the longer one. The second half
+continues the numbering, and pcre2_get_error_message() picks the half. */
+
+#define COMPILE_ERROR_TEXTS_SPLIT 60
+
+static const unsigned char compile_error_texts_60[] =
   /* 60 */
   "(*VERB) not recognized or malformed\0"
   "subpattern number is too big\0"
@@ -342,6 +352,11 @@
   {
   message = compile_error_texts;
   n = enumber - COMPILE_ERROR_BASE;
+  if (n >= COMPILE_ERROR_TEXTS_SPLIT)
+    {
+    message = compile_error_texts_60;
+    n -= COMPILE_ERROR_TEXTS_SPLIT;
+    }
   }
 else if (enumber < 0)               /* Match or UTF error */
   {
